    "body": "An axolotl looks a little like a tiny underwater dragon. It has a wide face, small feet, and feathery gills that stick out from the sides of its head. Those gills help it take oxygen from water. Axolotls are salamanders, a type of amphibian, but they spend their lives mostly in water instead of changing into land-dwelling adults. Their unusual appearance makes them popular in pictures and games. Their biology, however, is even more remarkable than their looks.\n\n## A home near Mexico City\n\nWild axolotls come from the lake and canal system around Xochimilco in Mexico. Long ago, a larger network of lakes covered parts of the region. As the city grew, much of that habitat changed. Water pollution, habitat loss, and introduced fish have threatened the small wild population. It is important to distinguish wild axolotls from those raised in laboratories or aquariums. Many people see captive axolotls, but that does not mean the species is safe in its natural home.\n\n## Staying young in an unusual way\n\nMost salamanders begin life in water and later change their bodies as they grow. Axolotls usually keep several features from their juvenile stage, including their external gills. Scientists call this neoteny. The animal still becomes capable of reproducing, even though its body retains features associated with younger salamanders. This unusual development helps biologists study how body systems change over time and why animals in the same group can follow different growth paths.\n\n## A surprising repair ability\n\nIf an axolotl loses part of a limb, it can grow that part back. Scientists call this regeneration. The process involves cells responding to injury, organizing new tissue, and rebuilding structures in the correct locations. The regenerated part is not made instantly, and the ability is not unlimited under every condition. Still, it is far beyond the kind of repair humans can normally perform. Researchers study axolotls to understand how cells coordinate such complex work.\n\n## Why this matters to people\n\nStudying regeneration does not mean scientists have discovered a way to give people new arms or legs. The process is complicated, and animal biology cannot simply be copied into human bodies. Researchers ask more careful questions. Which signals tell cells where to grow? How do new tissues connect with older ones? Why do some animals repair damage more completely than others? Scientific progress often begins with understanding these questions before anyone can imagine a medical application.\n\n## Why an unusual animal matters\n\nWhen scientists study an axolotl, they do not only look at its missing limb. They compare cells before an injury, during healing, and after new tissue begins to form. They use microscopes and carefully designed experiments to discover which processes change. A single observation is not enough to explain regeneration. Researchers need results from many animals and repeated tests. The conservation problem adds another question: how can people protect a rare wild species while studying its biology responsibly? Habitat protection and scientific research can both matter. The axolotl reminds readers that an animal can be important for more than being cute or famous on the internet. Its survival has value in its own ecosystem, regardless of whether people ever find a medical application.\n\n## Protecting a remarkable species\n\nA conservation project might monitor water quality, protect habitat, or reduce threats from introduced animals. Those actions are important because the axolotl's special abilities cannot save it from every danger. Students can learn by drawing an axolotl and labeling the gills, tail, and limbs. Then compare it with a frog or another amphibian. You will discover that nature's surprises often come from the many different ways living things grow, breathe, and survive.",
