    "title": "The Northern Lights: Why the Sky Sometimes Glows",
    "dek": "Charged particles from the Sun meet Earth's atmosphere and create moving curtains of light.",
    "sources": [
      "https://www.swpc.noaa.gov/phenomena/aurora",
      "https://science.nasa.gov/sun/auroras/"
    ],
    "body": "On some dark nights far from bright city lights, the sky glows with curtains of green, pink, or purple. These moving colors are auroras. In the Northern Hemisphere, people often call them the northern lights, while similar displays near the South Pole are called the southern lights. They look like paintings across the night sky, but auroras are produced by natural interactions between the Sun, Earth's magnetic field, and gases high in the atmosphere.\n\n## The Sun sends more than light\n\nThe Sun gives Earth light and warmth, but it also sends a stream of charged particles into space. This stream is known as the solar wind. The particles travel outward in many directions. Earth has a magnetic field that helps guide and deflect many charged particles. Some particles enter regions near the planet's magnetic poles and collide with gases high above the ground. Energy is transferred during these collisions, and the gases can emit visible light.\n\n## Why the colors change\n\nDifferent atmospheric gases and collision conditions can produce different colors. Oxygen is responsible for much of the green light people often notice in auroras. Other colors can appear at different heights or under different conditions. The lights may seem to ripple because the streams of particles and magnetic field lines are changing. A single display can shift from a pale band into curtains or arcs, sometimes within minutes.\n\n## A connection to space weather\n\nScientists use the term space weather for conditions near Earth that are affected by activity from the Sun. Stronger solar activity can sometimes make auroras visible farther from the poles than usual. But those conditions can also affect satellites, radio communications, and electrical systems. Researchers monitor the Sun and Earth's magnetic environment to help people prepare for unusual activity. Watching auroras is beautiful, but the science also has practical importance for technology.\n\n## Where and when to look\n\nAuroras are more common at high latitudes, including parts of Alaska, Canada, Scandinavia, and Antarctica. A dark sky and clear weather make them easier to see. During strong geomagnetic activity, people farther south may get a chance. Forecasts can suggest when conditions are promising, but no forecast guarantees a dramatic display at a particular place. Clouds, city lights, and the timing of solar activity all matter.\n\n## What makes an aurora photograph convincing?\n\nA photograph can show beautiful light, but it may not reveal when or where the picture was taken. Some cameras can collect light over several seconds, making colors appear brighter than they would to the human eye. That does not mean the aurora is fake. It means photographers can use settings that change the way light appears in an image. A careful news report should include the location and date of an aurora photograph, especially when claiming an unusual display occurred far from the poles. Scientists also compare photos with measurements of magnetic activity. Connecting observations with data helps explain why a glowing sky appeared at that time.\n\nThe colors themselves are evidence of energy interacting with gases in Earth's upper atmosphere, not paint or reflected city lights.\n\n## Make a model of the idea\n\nUse colored pencils to draw Earth with curved magnetic field lines around it. Add arrows for particles coming from the Sun and mark the areas near the poles where auroras often appear. The model will be simplified, but it can help explain why the most common auroras occur near certain parts of the planet. The next time someone shares a photo of the northern lights, you will know that the colors connect our atmosphere to activity on a star millions of miles away.",
