    "slug": "wind-turbines-turning-air-to-electricity",
    "section": "Science",
    "title": "How a Wind Turbine Turns Moving Air into Power",
    "dek": "Giant blades capture energy from the wind and help generate electricity for homes and communities.",
    "sources": [
      "https://www.energy.gov/eere/wind/how-do-wind-turbines-work",
      "https://www.energy.gov/eere/wind/wind-energy-basics"
    ],
    "body": "A wind turbine looks like a very tall fan, but it works in the opposite direction. A fan uses electricity to move air. A wind turbine uses moving air to help make electricity. You may see turbines standing across open fields or along coastlines. Their long blades rotate when enough wind passes across them. Inside the machine, that movement drives equipment that produces electrical energy. The process depends on physics, careful design, and the changing conditions of the weather.\n\n## The shape of a blade matters\n\nWind turbine blades are shaped somewhat like airplane wings. As air moves around a blade, forces make it turn. The blades are connected to a rotor, which turns a shaft. A generator converts the mechanical motion into electrical energy. Some turbine designs use additional equipment to adjust the speed or direction of movement. The basic idea is that one form of energy, the motion of the air, is transformed into another form people can use.\n\n## Why turbines are so tall\n\nWind often blows more strongly and steadily higher above the ground because buildings, trees, and hills can slow and disturb air near the surface. Placing a turbine high on a tower can help it reach better wind conditions. The length of the blades also matters. Longer blades sweep through a larger area, giving the machine access to more moving air. However, very large turbines require strong materials, careful transportation, and safe installation.\n\n## Wind is not constant\n\nSome days are breezy and others are calm. A wind turbine makes different amounts of electricity depending on the wind. Grid operators, the people and systems managing electricity networks, must plan around these changes. Electricity can also come from solar panels, water turbines, natural gas plants, nuclear plants, and other sources. A reliable power system may combine several sources. Batteries and other forms of storage can help shift energy from times of high supply to times of demand.\n\n## What people consider when placing turbines\n\nEngineers and communities study wind speed, land use, wildlife, cost, and distance to electrical connections. A good site needs enough wind to make the project worthwhile. Designers also study how turbines affect birds and bats and look for ways to reduce harm. People may have different views about where turbines should be built. Those decisions involve benefits and trade-offs that deserve evidence and careful discussion.\n\n## Energy does not appear from nowhere\n\nWind power illustrates the idea that energy changes form. Moving air has kinetic energy, or energy of motion. The turbine's blades transfer part of that motion into rotation, and a generator converts the mechanical energy into electricity. Not all the wind's energy becomes useful electricity. Friction and other effects cause losses, and the turbine cannot capture every bit of moving air. Engineers measure efficiency to compare how effectively designs work. A student can make a diagram with arrows showing where energy begins and where it goes. The same idea applies to bicycles, electric fans, water turbines, and many other machines. Understanding energy transformations helps people see technology as connected systems.\n\nTurbines also need regular maintenance, which includes checking mechanical parts so the equipment works safely and reliably.\n\n## Investigate moving air\n\nMake a simple paper pinwheel with an adult, place it where a gentle breeze can reach it, and observe how fast it turns. Try changing the direction you face, but avoid standing near roads or hazardous equipment. The pinwheel does not generate electricity, yet it demonstrates the first part of a turbine's job: capturing motion from moving air. Wind power is one example of how people use science to turn natural energy into useful technology.",
