    "slug": "gps-satellites-help-you-navigate",
    "section": "Science",
    "title": "How Your Phone Knows Where It Is",
    "dek": "Satellites, radio signals, and careful timing help a map find your position.",
    "sources": [
      "https://www.gps.gov/systems/gps/",
      "https://www.nasa.gov/missions/"
    ],
    "body": "You open a map on a phone and see a blue dot marking your location. It may seem as if the phone simply knows where you are. In reality, finding a position can involve signals from satellites high above Earth, mathematical calculations, and information from nearby networks. GPS, short for Global Positioning System, is one important part of that process. It was developed by the United States and is used for navigation in cars, on ships, and in many other settings.\n\n## Satellites as moving reference points\n\nGPS satellites travel around Earth in predictable paths. Each sends radio signals that include information about its position and the time the signal was sent. A receiver, such as one inside a phone, can compare when different signals arrive. Because radio signals travel at a known speed, the receiver can estimate how far away a satellite is. Using information from multiple satellites helps it calculate a position on or near Earth.\n\n## Why time must be accurate\n\nImagine measuring the distance of a runner by counting seconds. If your clock were inaccurate, your answer would also be inaccurate. GPS relies on extremely precise time measurements because radio signals move close to the speed of light. Even a tiny timing error can lead to a large distance error. Satellites use accurate clocks, and the system accounts for effects that would otherwise make measurements less reliable. This is a remarkable example of how everyday technology depends on precise science.\n\n## More than one kind of location signal\n\nA phone may also use nearby Wi-Fi networks and cellular towers to help estimate its position, especially where satellite reception is weak. Tall buildings, tunnels, and other obstacles can interfere with signals. That is why a location dot may sometimes drift or appear in the wrong place. A navigation app can give useful directions, but it is not perfect. People still need to pay attention to signs, roads, and their surroundings.\n\n## The mathematics of position\n\nA receiver needs signals from several satellites because one distance measurement does not identify a unique location. Many points could be the same distance from one satellite. More measurements reduce the possibilities. This process is related to trilateration, finding a position using distances from known points. A simple classroom model might use circles drawn around several dots to show where possible locations overlap.\n\n## GPS and privacy\n\nA phone can calculate location without announcing it to everyone, but apps may ask for permission to use that information. Location data can be sensitive because it may reveal where a person lives, studies, or travels. Responsible digital habits include reviewing which apps can access location and avoiding sharing a precise position publicly. Some apps need location to provide directions; others may work without it. The science of GPS describes how a receiver finds its position, while privacy choices determine what happens to that information afterward. Those are two separate but important questions. A useful technology becomes safer when people understand both how it functions and how its data may be used.\n\nDifferent navigation systems can work together, helping phones estimate a position even as conditions change.\n\n## Try a map challenge\n\nFind three landmarks near your school on a map. Imagine that you know how far away you are from each landmark but not which direction you face. Draw circles representing those distances and look for where they meet. Real GPS is far more complex, but the model introduces the idea behind location calculations. The next time you use navigation, remember that the tiny dot on the screen depends on satellites, clocks, mathematics, and engineers working together.",
